docker network
-----------------
We need Docker networks so containers can communicate with each other and with the outside world.
to run in the same port means we can try by running in different network  but port forwarding is recommended first
Think about a real application:
Frontend
   ↓
Backend
   ↓
Database

If these are separate Docker containers, they need a way to communicate.
Without a Docker network
Container 1          Container 2
Frontend             Backend
   ❌                 ❌
      Cannot easily communicate
With a Docker network
             Docker Network
          ┌──────────────────┐
          │                  │
     Frontend ───────→ Backend
                         │
                         ↓
                     Database
          └──────────────────┘
Now they can communicate.
bridge: Default choice for containers on one host.
host: Uses the host's network directly.  Host network = Don't create a separate Docker network for the container; use the host machine's network directly.
none: No networking.
overlay: Multi-host networking for Docker Swarm.
macvlan: Containers appear as devices on the physical network.
Use user-defined bridge networks for automatic DNS-based service discovery and better isolation.

when you use bridge
Host IP       = 10.0.1.10
Container 1   = 172.17.0.2
Container 2   = 172.17.0.3

bridge → container gets its own Docker network/IP
host   → container uses host's network directly
none   → container has no network

docker network ls   List networks
docker network create mynetwork               Create a network   default bridge
docker network create --driver bridge sunil       sunl is a network name
docker network inspect mynetwork                Inspect network
docker run -d --name app --network mynetwork nginx      Run container inside a network
docker network rm mynetwork    Remove network
docker network prune           Remove unused networks
docker network connect my-network container-name     Connect an existing container to a network
docker network disconnect my-network container-name   Disconnect a container

Suppose you already have a container: docker run -d --name app nginx  Now connect that existing app container to my-network:
docker network connect my-network app 

If your container is currently connected to another network and you want it on your network, you have two choices.
1. Add it to your network     docker network connect my-network container1
 Now the container is connected to both networks:
old-network
     |
  container1
     |
my-network
2. Remove it from the old network  If you want it to be only on your network:
docker network disconnect old-network container1
Then:my-network
     |
  container1
So, to move a container from one network to another:
docker network connect my-network container1
docker network disconnect old-network container1
Important: Docker doesn't have a single move command. You connect first, then disconnect.

docker network connect my-network app
So:
my-network → the network you created
app → the existing container
After this, the app container becomes part of my-network and can communicate with other containers connected to the same network.
Simple example
my-network
   |
   +---- app
   |
   +---- database

Now app can communicate with database using the container name:
Now app can communicate with database using the container name:  app → database
You use docker network connect when the container already exists. If you are creating the container now, you can directly use:
docker run -d --name app --network my-network nginx


If you mean host network, it means:
The container uses the host machine's network directly.
Normally:
Host
  |
 Docker network
  |
Container

With host:

Host network
     |
  Container
The container does not get its own separate IP address from Docker.
Example:
docker run -d --network host nginx
If Nginx listens on port 80, it uses the host's port 80 directly.

It means the container shares the network of the machine where Docker is running.

For example, suppose:

AWS EC2 Ubuntu
IP = 10.0.1.20

Normally with bridge:

EC2 (10.0.1.20)
      |
   Docker
      |
 Container (separate Docker IP)

With host network:

EC2 (10.0.1.20)
      |
   Container

The container uses the same network interfaces and IP as the EC2 host.
So if your container runs Nginx on port 80:
docker run -d --network host nginx
Nginx uses the EC2's port 80 directly.


We use Docker host network when we want the container to use the host's network directly, mainly to avoid Docker's network isolation/NAT.
Why use it?
1. Better network performance
With bridge:  Container → Docker network → Host network → Internet
With host:    Container → Host network → Internet
There is less Docker networking overhead.

2. When the application needs the host's network
For example, a network-monitoring application may need to see the host's network interfaces directly.

3. Simpler port handling
With bridge, you usually do:
docker run -p 8080:80 nginx
With host:  docker run --network host nginx
If Nginx listens on port 80, it uses the host's port 80 directly.
Important
You don't normally use host networking for every container.
For normal applications: Frontend → bridge network → Backend → bridge network → Database
is usually better because it provides network isolation.


If you're asking which one provides network isolation: Host or Bridge?
👉 Bridge provides network isolation.

Bridge
Host
 |
 +--- Docker Bridge Network
       |
       +--- Container A
       +--- Container B

Containers have their own Docker network/IP, separated from the host's network.
Host
Host Network
     |
  Container
The container shares the host's network, so there is less network isolation.
So remember:
Bridge → network isolation ✅
Host → no separate network isolation ❌


none means the container has NO network connection.
Example: docker run -it --network none ubuntu
The container will have:
Container
   |
   X
No network

It cannot normally communicate with:

❌ Internet
❌ Other containers
❌ Host through Docker networking
Why use none?
When you want maximum network isolation.
For example, if a container only needs to process some files locally and doesn't need any network access, you can use:
docker run --network none my-app

